[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JavaScript Proxy: A Deep Dive"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The JavaScript Proxy object enables you to create a proxy for another object (the target). The proxy can intercept and redefine fundamental operations for the target object. These operations include property lookup, assignment, enumeration, function invocation, and more. This provides powerful metaprogramming capabilities."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Proxy Creation"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Proxy is created using the `Proxy()` constructor, which takes two arguments:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The target object: The object which you want to proxy."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The handler object: An object that defines which operations will be intercepted and how to redefine intercepted operations."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"const target = {};
const handler = {};
const proxy = new Proxy(target, handler);

proxy.a = 1;
console.log(target.a); // Output: 1"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this simple example, the `handler` is an empty object, so all operations are forwarded directly to the `target` object. Setting `proxy.a` also sets `target.a`."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Handler Methods (Traps)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `handler` object contains methods called "traps". Each trap intercepts a specific operation on the target object. Some common traps include:"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`get(target, propKey, receiver)`: Intercepts property access (e.g., `proxy.prop` or `proxy['prop']`)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`set(target, propKey, value, receiver)`: Intercepts property assignment (e.g., `proxy.prop = value`)."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`has(target, propKey)`: Intercepts the `in` operator (e.g., `'prop' in proxy`)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`deleteProperty(target, propKey)`: Intercepts the `delete` operator (e.g., `delete proxy.prop`)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`apply(target, thisArg, argumentsList)`: Intercepts function calls (only if the target is a function)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`construct(target, argumentsList, newTarget)`: Intercepts the `new` operator (only if the target is a constructor function)."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Using the `get` Trap"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"const target = {
  name: 'John Doe',
  age: 30
};

const handler = {
  get: function(target, propKey, receiver) {
    console.log(`Getting property ${propKey}!`);
    return Reflect.get(target, propKey, receiver);
  }
};

const proxy = new Proxy(target, handler);

console.log(proxy.name); // Output: Getting property name!\n// John Doe"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `get` trap logs a message whenever a property is accessed. `Reflect.get()` is used to forward the operation to the target object.  Using `Reflect` methods is crucial for maintaining the expected behavior and context."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Using the `set` Trap for Validation"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"const target = {};

const handler = {
  set: function(target, propKey, value, receiver) {
    if (typeof value !== 'number') {
      throw new TypeError('Property must be a number');
    }
    target[propKey] = value;
    return true; // Indicate success
  }
};

const proxy = new Proxy(target, handler);

proxy.age = 30; // Works fine

try {
  proxy.age = 'thirty'; // Throws TypeError
} catch (e) {
  console.error(e);
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, the `set` trap validates that the assigned value is a number. If not, it throws a `TypeError`. The `set` trap must return `true` to indicate successful assignment."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Revocable Proxies"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"A revocable proxy is a proxy that can be disabled. After a proxy has been revoked, any operations performed on it will throw a `TypeError`. Revocable proxies are created using `Proxy.revocable()`."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"const { proxy, revoke } = Proxy.revocable({}, {
  get: function(target, propKey) {
    return 'Accessed!';
  }
});

console.log(proxy.a); // Output: Accessed!

revoke();

try {
  console.log(proxy.a); // Throws TypeError
} catch (e) {
  console.error(e);
}"},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Use Cases"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Validation: Enforcing data types or constraints on property assignments."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Logging/Debugging: Tracking property access or modifications."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Virtualization: Creating virtual objects that load data on demand."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Security: Controlling access to sensitive properties."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Binding: Implementing data binding mechanisms in frameworks."},{"order":32,"type":"subheading","text":"Important Considerations"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Proxies introduce a layer of indirection, which can impact performance. Use them judiciously, especially in performance-critical sections of your code. Also, be mindful of the complexity they add to your code. Ensure that the benefits outweigh the added complexity."}]
